{
  "name": "an opening reaches a contributor through any one entry that admits them",
  "description": "ADR-0024 decision 5, ADR-0020 amendment 1 and ADR-0021 amendment 1: the worked example of RFC-0024 §2, all five rows, plus the contract case beside it.\n\nAn entry ADMITS a contributor by place: remote-anywhere admits everybody, remote-in-a-country admits whoever states that country, and ONSITE ADMITS EVERYBODY — including somebody who has stated no country at all. A role reaches a contributor when ANY entry admits them. For a full-time role an entry counts only if its mode is one the contributor ticked (open_to_remote / open_to_onsite); the other engagements read their own flag and then need any one entry to admit them. This gates GET /openings and nothing else — a hirer's search is a preference they edit (ADR-0021).\n\nOne full-time role, onsite in Bengaluru and remote in India, published with no bar so that place and shape are the only things deciding. Alice's profile is replaced before each read:\n\n| in India, remote only | yes — the remote-in-India entry |\n| in Germany, remote only | no — the remote entry is India only, and onsite is not ticked |\n| in Germany, onsite | yes — the onsite entry |\n| no country, onsite | yes — the onsite entry admits an unstated country |\n| no country, remote only | no — an unstated country is not India |\n\nThen the same two entries on a CONTRACT role reach somebody in Germany who ticked contract and neither full-time mode: the onsite entry admits them, and the contract flag is the shape that counts. `missed` counts every live opening not shown, whatever the reason, as it did before (RFC-0024 §6 row 1).\n\nThe contributor reads each entry at their level of detail (decision 7): a country for remote, a city and a country for onsite, and never a street, a postcode or an address id — ordered remote first, then by country, then by city, as the hirer read is (amendment 1 §1.7).",
